#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500329 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152157 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix For a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f0116c-d24c-501d-a2e3-6d64ae73f071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499660} {:answer/id #uuid "4f094944-ced0-5da6-beff-36966c6a9bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499804} {:answer/id #uuid "5899b9f1-4d49-51f2-8d77-aac18ae3041c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499825} {:answer/id #uuid "84d56836-dc1c-5c3e-88b2-8e486dbb45fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499910} {:answer/id #uuid "aacd61b0-026b-5712-a0a0-1b3c6315b32a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84d56836-dc1c-5c3e-88b2-8e486dbb45fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499910} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffacf0a3-cce6-5d50-befc-250fcc891555" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "94724c66-b6e2-52f7-8122-f7efcf9307aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-111515" :question/problem "The matrix $V$ is given below. Let ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$,  ${v}_{3}$, and ${v}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $V.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${v}_{4}={x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 8 & 12\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $V$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 8 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 8 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 6 & 18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 6 & 18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${v}_{4}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{4}}={{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+{{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=3\\cdot 3=9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152157 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix For a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "046a6bcc-7129-549e-9c2d-1feee3707139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244350} {:answer/id #uuid "8086baec-7c98-5738-a5e5-8bb3cf74e486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245447} {:answer/id #uuid "89e4aa92-ad36-5d2c-b52f-f0b8ad6123f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245522} {:answer/id #uuid "95f5d6c6-be27-5bdb-a4f3-ec59918f9616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245632} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc8b2c9-de7b-5b6a-8d60-6e3dd551afe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\{{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(R)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdc8b2c9-de7b-5b6a-8d60-6e3dd551afe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\{{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(R)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500042} :answer-field/id #uuid "758b5fc0-bb5f-54aa-bd3c-eef9e30dca1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4d5f352a-5b0e-55b2-a382-bea7929379c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-111807" :question/problem "The matrix $R$ is given below. Let ${r}_{1}$, ${r}_{2}$,  ${r}_{3}$ and ${r}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $R.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(R).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${r}_{4}={x}_{1}{r}_{1}+{x}_{2}{r}_{2}+{x}_{3}{r}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 & -10\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $R$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -10 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(R)$ is $\\{{r}_{1},{r}_{2}\\}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B=\\{{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(R).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152156 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix With Two Rows"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a36f86a-9cf0-5a74-9e73-0b6b6057dd12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149988} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e7f094-f20a-53ac-ad68-c1b836150308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149989} {:answer/id #uuid "15a8bf83-6bed-5f2d-a921-4c1c41806500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149991} {:answer/id #uuid "39f0bda6-54c7-5b33-a8c3-6a85937caa55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149992} {:answer/id #uuid "1a09c2a0-736e-5081-9d7b-cd940e10ce12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{a}_{1},{a}_{3}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39f0bda6-54c7-5b33-a8c3-6a85937caa55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149992} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d955d5f-294e-53d0-813c-277787b98f85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40c35237-5ebd-5668-8d9f-8241b6761c15" :question/math-academy-id "q-34510" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and ${a}_{3}$ are the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{a}_{1},{a}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${a}_{2}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 13\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{13}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${a}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}={{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{{(0)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=3\\cdot 0=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152156 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix With Two Rows"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016024ef-c03f-5e15-8220-be9fb68e1bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342260} {:answer/id #uuid "094fd71c-9a23-5d25-860d-bc340d895bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342315} {:answer/id #uuid "3e7a7b2d-982a-5570-b5f8-b8e34bf4d03c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342753} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed580a2-4268-5399-96f5-ea373b9218d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343193} {:answer/id #uuid "8255c71a-6cc1-58b7-8b63-f24165915a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(P)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e7a7b2d-982a-5570-b5f8-b8e34bf4d03c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342753} :answer-field/id #uuid "3033d739-a4dc-5f2c-8494-cf265f068411" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "edfd11b7-178d-5b9f-89aa-f777ae4db963" :question/math-academy-id "q-75946" :question/problem "The matrix $P$ is given below. Let ${p}_{1}$, ${p}_{2}$, and ${p}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $P.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(P).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${p}_{3}={x}_{1}{p}_{1}+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $P$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(P)$ is $\\{{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${p}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{3}}={{(-2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{1}}+{{(1)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-2)\\cdot 1=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152156 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix With Two Rows"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12117f66-7e89-5080-9c67-2baf0fc5322d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483598} {:answer/id #uuid "4c340483-1504-5c29-8184-e2464a93b17c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483708} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecb7b58-6f98-517c-8a10-0c822f40ce2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483826} {:answer/id #uuid "b17ad927-4efe-5a54-8fec-c33efe368201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483920} {:answer/id #uuid "b5f2c10c-efb5-5f13-bc42-613ef41e630b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12117f66-7e89-5080-9c67-2baf0fc5322d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483598} :answer-field/id #uuid "64759caf-77ed-5792-a395-f9662dbea5af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e023a9e-c79a-53ca-9a76-a188bd7ab902" :question/math-academy-id "q-75951" :question/problem "The matrix $Q$ is given below. Let ${q}_{1}$, ${q}_{2}$, and ${q}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $Q.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${q}_{3}={x}_{1}{q}_{1}+{x}_{2}{q}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $Q$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$ is $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${q}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{3}}={{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{1}}+{{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 2=4.$"}]}
